@page
@model AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About IPFees";
}

<div class="py-2">
    <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold mb-3">About IPFees</h1>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 46rem;">
        IPFees calculates official filing, examination, and renewal fees for PCT national and regional
        phase entry across 119 patent jurisdictions. More to the point of this page: it's a working
        demonstration of
        <a href="https://github.com/vbocan/IPFLang" target="_blank">IPFLang</a>, the domain-specific
        language it runs on. If you want to see what a statically-verified, currency-typed DSL for
        regulatory fee computation looks like in practice &mdash; not in a paper, in a browser &mdash;
        this is the tool to poke at.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 text-uppercase text-secondary mt-5 mb-3">What it actually does</h2>
    <div style="max-width: 46rem;">
        <p>
            Pick one or more jurisdictions, answer a short questionnaire &mdash; entity size, claim count,
            filing date, whatever that jurisdiction's schedule asks for &mdash; and get a total in the
            currency of your choice. Alongside the total you get the full rule trace: every condition the
            engine evaluated, including the ones that didn't apply and why, so the number on screen is
            never a black box.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every fee schedule behind this is a plain-text <code>.ipf</code> script, not business logic
            buried in C#. You can read the ones this instance ships with on
            <a href="https://github.com/vbocan/IPFLang/tree/master/jurisdictions" target="_blank">GitHub</a>,
            or write your own in the <a asp-page="/Admin">Backstage</a> sandbox.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h4 text-uppercase text-secondary mt-5 mb-3">Why a language, and not a spreadsheet</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 46rem;">
        Government fee schedules are legal text with a lot of conditional structure &mdash; reduced rates
        for small entities, per-claim surcharges past a threshold, examination fees that depend on how the
        search was handled. Hardcoding that into an application means every fee change is a code change and
        a deployment. IPFLang keeps the rules as data a non-programmer can read and edit, while still giving
        you the two things a spreadsheet can't: proof that the rules are complete, and a type system that
        catches currency mistakes before they reach a number.
    </p>

    <div class="row g-4 mt-1 mb-5" style="max-width: 62rem;">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="d-flex">
                <div class="flex-shrink-0">
                    <div class="rounded-circle bg-primary bg-opacity-10 text-primary d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center" style="width:52px;height:52px;">
                        <i class="bi bi-patch-check fs-4"></i>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ms-3">
                    <h3 class="h6 fw-bold mb-1">Statically verified schedules</h3>
                    <p class="text-secondary mb-0">
                        A script can declare <code>VERIFY COMPLETE</code> or <code>VERIFY MONOTONIC</code>.
                        The editor checks it on save &mdash; <code>COMPLETE</code> means every input
                        combination is covered, <code>MONOTONIC</code> means the fee never moves the wrong
                        way against an input you name. A schedule that fails either is refused, with the
                        exact gap named.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="d-flex">
                <div class="flex-shrink-0">
                    <div class="rounded-circle bg-primary bg-opacity-10 text-primary d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center" style="width:52px;height:52px;">
                        <i class="bi bi-currency-exchange fs-4"></i>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ms-3">
                    <h3 class="h6 fw-bold mb-1">Currency-safe by construction</h3>
                    <p class="text-secondary mb-0">
                        Every monetary literal carries its currency. Adding 100&nbsp;EUR to 50&nbsp;USD is a
                        compile-time type error, not a runtime surprise &mdash; you convert explicitly, and
                        that conversion stays visible in the script.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="d-flex">
                <div class="flex-shrink-0">
                    <div class="rounded-circle bg-primary bg-opacity-10 text-primary d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center" style="width:52px;height:52px;">
                        <i class="bi bi-list-check fs-4"></i>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ms-3">
                    <h3 class="h6 fw-bold mb-1">Full calculation provenance</h3>
                    <p class="text-secondary mb-0">
                        Every result ships with the complete rule trace &mdash; what fired, what didn't and
                        why, and how each piece contributed to the total. A charge you didn't incur is shown
                        as clearly as one you did.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <div class="d-flex">
                <div class="flex-shrink-0">
                    <div class="rounded-circle bg-primary bg-opacity-10 text-primary d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center" style="width:52px;height:52px;">
                        <i class="bi bi-unlock fs-4"></i>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="ms-3">
                    <h3 class="h6 fw-bold mb-1">Open by default</h3>
                    <p class="text-secondary mb-0">
                        The schedules, the calculator, and the REST API are all public, no key required. The
                        <a asp-page="/Admin">Backstage</a> sandbox lets you add jurisdictions and edit fees
                        directly against a database that resets every six hours &mdash; nothing you do there
                        is permanent, so there's nothing to be careful about.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h4 text-uppercase text-secondary mt-5 mb-3">How this relates to IPFLang</h2>
    <div style="max-width: 46rem;">
        <p>
            The language began inside this repository as an internal calculator component. It was pulled
            out, given a formal grammar, a currency-aware type system, and static verification, and now
            ships separately as <a href="https://github.com/vbocan/IPFLang" target="_blank">IPFLang</a>.
            IPFees consumes it as a versioned package &mdash; it doesn't implement the language, it's the
            deployable layer around it: the web interface, the REST API, multi-currency conversion with
            historical rates, and the 119-jurisdiction corpus.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
            Concretely, that split means the interesting engine work &mdash; the parser, the type checker,
            the verifier &mdash; lives in a general-purpose, reusable library with its own test suite and
            its own releases, while IPFees stays focused on being a good calculator. See the
            <a asp-page="/Reference">language reference</a> and <a asp-page="/Grammar">formal grammar</a> for
            what that engine actually accepts.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h4 text-uppercase text-secondary mt-5 mb-3">Try it yourself</h2>
    <div style="max-width: 46rem;">
        <ul class="list-unstyled">
            <li class="mb-2"><i class="bi bi-calculator text-primary me-2"></i><a asp-area="" asp-page="/Index">Run a calculation</a> across any of the 119 jurisdictions</li>
            <li class="mb-2"><i class="bi bi-book text-primary me-2"></i><a asp-page="/Reference">Read the language reference</a> for IPFLang's syntax, with examples for every construct</li>
            <li class="mb-2"><i class="bi bi-diagram-2 text-primary me-2"></i><a asp-page="/Grammar">Read the formal grammar</a> if you want the EBNF instead of prose</li>
            <li class="mb-2"><i class="bi bi-wrench-adjustable text-primary me-2"></i><a asp-page="/Admin">Explore Backstage</a>, the sandbox where you can write and run your own schedules</li>
            <li class="mb-2"><i class="bi bi-cloud text-primary me-2"></i><a href="https://ipfeesapi.dataman.ro/swagger/index.html" target="_blank">Call the API</a> directly &mdash; it's the same data the web calculator uses</li>
            <li class="mb-2"><i class="bi bi-github text-primary me-2"></i><a href="https://github.com/vbocan/ipfees" target="_blank">Read the source</a>, file an issue, or send a pull request for a jurisdiction that's missing</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h4 text-uppercase text-secondary mt-5 mb-3">Where this honestly stands</h2>
    <div style="max-width: 46rem;">
        <p>
            This is research software, not a commercial product, and it's priced accordingly &mdash; free,
            with no SLA. IP practitioners at <strong>Jet IP</strong> reviewed a sample of calculations
            against official fee schedules and caught real discrepancies before this went live; that's the
            extent of external review so far, not a formal audit. Treat the hosted instance at
            <a href="https://ipfees.dataman.ro/" target="_blank">ipfees.dataman.ro</a> as a reference
            service, and run your own deployment if you need one as a dependency.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
            An earlier version of the API carried an <code>[ApiKey]</code> attribute that never actually
            enforced anything &mdash; the validator behind it accepted every request, key or no key. Rather
            than leave a control that looks present and isn't, it was removed; the API is documented as open
            because that's what it is.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h4 text-uppercase text-secondary mt-5 mb-3">Research, licensing, and citation</h2>
    <div style="max-width: 46rem;">
        <p>
            IPFees is developed by <a href="//www.bocan.ro" target="_blank">Dr. Valer Bocan</a> at
            Politehnica University of Timișoara, as part of ongoing research into domain-specific languages
            for regulatory computation. IPFLang itself is described in a peer-reviewed article in the
            <em>IEEE Open Journal of the Computer Society</em>, co-authored with Dr. Valentina E. Balas.
        </p>
        <p>
            Both IPFees and IPFLang are open source under the
            <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0" target="_blank">GNU GPL v3.0</a>. Fork either
            one, deploy your own instance, or send a pull request. If you use IPFees in research, citation
            metadata is in
            <a href="https://github.com/vbocan/ipfees/blob/master/CITATION.cff" target="_blank">CITATION.cff</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
